## Checklist

- [ ] Define ports as interfaces
- [ ] Implement domain without external dependencies
- [ ] Create application services using ports
- [ ] Implement driving adapters (UI)
- [ ] Implement driven adapters (external systems)
- [ ] Wire dependencies properly
- [ ] Test ports in isolation
- [ ] Test adapters independently
- [ ] Keep domain pure
- [ ] Document ports

## Anti Patterns

### 1. Leaking Infrastructure into Domain

```typescript
// Không nên - infrastructure in domain
class Order {
    saveToDatabase() {
        const db = new PostgresConnection();
        db.query('INSERT INTO orders...');
    }
}

// Nên - domain is pure
class Order {
    // Only domain logic
}
```

### 2. Missing Ports

```typescript
// Không nên - direct dependency
class OrderService {
    constructor(
        private db: PostgresConnection,  // Too concrete
        private stripe: Stripe          // Too concrete
    ) {}
}

// Nên - use ports
class OrderService {
    constructor(
        private repository: IOrderRepository,    // Port
        private paymentGateway: IPaymentGateway // Port
    ) {}
}
```

### 3. Heavy Application Layer

```typescript
// Không nên - application layer does everything
class OrderService {
    async createOrder(input) {
        // Validation logic
        // Business logic
        // Database operations
        // Payment processing
        // Notification sending
        // All in one!
    }
}

// Nên - delegate to domain
class OrderService {
    async createOrder(input) {
        const order = this.createOrderDomain(input);
        order.confirm(this.paymentGateway.charge(...));
        this.repository.save(order);
    }
}
```

## Best Practices

### 1. Single Responsibility per Adapter

```typescript
// One adapter per port
class PostgresOrderRepository implements IOrderRepository {}
class MongoOrderRepository implements IOrderRepository {}

// One adapter per external service
class StripePaymentGateway implements IPaymentGateway {}
class PayPalPaymentGateway implements IPaymentGateway {}
```

### 2. Immutable Domain

```typescript
// Domain objects should be immutable or have controlled mutations
class Order {
    private _status: OrderStatus;

    confirm(): void {
        if (this._status !== OrderStatus.DRAFT) {
            throw new BusinessRuleViolation();
        }
        this._status = OrderStatus.CONFIRMED;
    }
}
```

### 3. Async Adapters

```typescript
// Adapters can be sync or async
interface IOrderRepository {
    save(order: Order): Promise<void>;  // Async
}

interface ICacheAdapter {
    get<T>(key: string): Promise<T | null>;  // Async
}
```

## Workflow

### 1. Design Phase

1. Identify domain model
2. Define driving ports (use cases)
3. Define driven ports (external systems)
4. Plan adapters needed

### 2. Implementation

1. Implement domain layer
2. Implement ports (interfaces)
3. Implement application services
4. Implement adapters
5. Wire dependencies

### 3. Testing

1. Unit test domain
2. Unit test application services
3. Integration test adapters

## Ví dụ

### Ví dụ 1: Complete Adapter Example

```typescript
// adapters/secondary/persistence/InMemoryOrderRepository.ts
export class InMemoryOrderRepository implements IOrderRepository {
    private orders: Map<string, Order> = new Map();

    async save(order: Order): Promise<void> {
        this.orders.set(order.id.value, order);
    }

    async findById(id: OrderId): Promise<Order | null> {
        return this.orders.get(id.value) || null;
    }

    async findByCustomer(customerId: CustomerId): Promise<Order[]> {
        return Array.from(this.orders.values())
            .filter(o => o.customerId.equals(customerId));
    }

    async findByStatus(status: OrderStatus): Promise<Order[]> {
        return Array.from(this.orders.values())
            .filter(o => o.status.equals(status));
    }

    async delete(id: OrderId): Promise<void> {
        this.orders.delete(id.value);
    }
}

// Usage in tests
describe('OrderApplicationService', () => {
    let service: OrderApplicationService;
    const repository = new InMemoryOrderRepository();

    beforeEach(() => {
        service = new OrderApplicationService(
            repository,           // In-memory for tests
            new MockPaymentGateway(),
            new MockNotification(),
            new MockPublisher()
        );
    });

    it('should work with in-memory repository', async () => {
        const order = await service.createOrder(testInput());
        const retrieved = await service.getOrder(order.id);
        expect(retrieved).not.toBeNull();
    });
});
